// Thinking Process:
// 1. High-Yield Concept: Antiprotozoal pharmacology centers on a common thread — most agents
//    (nitroimidazoles, pentavalent antimonials, melarsoprol, etc.) generate reactive intermediates
//    that damage parasite DNA / proteins / membranes. Specific drugs map to specific organisms:
//    metronidazole + paromomycin for amoebiasis (tissue vs luminal); fexinidazole / pentamidine /
//    NECT / suramin / melarsoprol for HAT; benznidazole / nifurtimox for Chagas; pentavalent
//    antimonials, liposomal amphotericin B, miltefosine, paromomycin for leishmaniasis;
//    spiramycin (1st trimester) and pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + leucovorin for toxoplasmosis.
// 2. The "Trap": Confusing tissue versus luminal amebicides; conflating Gambiense versus
//    Rhodesiense HAT regimens; choosing melarsoprol for early-stage disease (its arsenical
//    toxicity restricts it to late-stage Rhodesiense); forgetting that spiramycin (not
//    pyrimethamine) is used <14 weeks gestation because pyrimethamine is a DHFR inhibitor and
//    teratogenic; miltefosine teratogenicity in pregnancy.
// 3. Key Distractor: "Right drug, wrong patient" — pentamidine vs suramin in pediatric HAT;
//    benznidazole's photosensitivity vs nifurtimox's GI/CNS effects; suramin + Onchocerca
//    coinfection risk; melarsoprol reactive encephalopathy.
// 4. Vignette Strategy: Returned travelers, sub-Saharan exposure, immunocompromised hosts
//    (HIV/AIDS cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptosporidial diarrhea), pregnant patients, liver
//    abscess scenarios. Mechanism withheld → forces student to reason from AE pattern or
//    pathophysiology to drug identity or mechanism class.
//
